'''Disney XD''' is an American [[pay television]] [[television channel|channel]] owned by the [[Disney Kids & Family]] and [[Disney Entertainment]] units of [[The Walt Disney Company]]. The channel is aimed primarily at older children ages six to eleven years old.<ref name="press">{{cite web |url=https://www.dgepress.com/waltdisneytelevision/about-us/disney-channels/ |title=Disney Channels - About Us |access-date=22 March 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210121050804/https://www.dgepress.com/waltdisneytelevision/about-us/disney-channels/ |archive-date=21 January 2021}}</ref>

Disney XD's programming consists of original first-run [[children's television series|television series]], current and former original series and [[Television film|made-for-television films]] inherited from [[sister station|sister network]] [[Disney Channel]], theatrically released films, and acquired programs from other distributors, along with a primetime block of programming involving [[Esports|competitive gaming]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Disney XD launches gaming-themed block |url=https://kidscreen.com/2017/06/15/disney-xd-launches-gaming-programming-block/ |access-date=2024-08-02 |archive-date=July 17, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250717091223/https://kidscreen.com/2017/06/15/disney-xd-launches-gaming-programming-block/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

{{As of|2023|11}}, Disney XD is available to approximately 44,000,000 pay television households in the United States, down from its 2013 peak of 82,000,000 households.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://wrestlenomics.com/u-s-cable-network-households-universe-1990-2023-nielsen-data/|title=U.S. cable network households (universe), 1990 – 2023|website=wrestlenomics.com|date=May 14, 2024|access-date=May 15, 2024}}</ref> In recent years, Disney XD's carriage has declined with the growth of streaming alternatives including its parent company's [[Disney+]], and has generally been depreciated by Disney in current retransmission consent negotiations with cable and streaming providers. Notably, the channel was removed in negotiations with [[Verizon Fios]] in 2025, and was unavailable on [[Charter Spectrum]] from 2023 to 2025.

==History==
[[File:2009 Disney XD alternate logo.svg|thumb|left|200px|Logo used from February 13, 2009 to June 1, 2015]]
Disney XD was launched on February 13, 2009, at 12:00&nbsp;a.m. [[Eastern Time Zone|Eastern Time]], with the ''[[Phineas and Ferb]]'' episode "[[Dude, We're Getting the Band Back Together]]" being the first show to air on the channel. The channel premiered its first original series, ''[[Aaron Stone]]'', at 7:00&nbsp;p.m. New animated series included in the channel's initial lineup were ''[[Kid vs. Kat]]'' and ''[[Jimmy Two-Shoes]]''.<ref>{{cite news|last=Haugsted |first=Linda |url=http://www.multichannel.com/article/161701-Disney_XD_Unwraps_On_Friday_The_13_th.php |title=Disney XD Unwraps on Friday the 13th ''(Archive)'' |date=January 7, 2009 |work=MultiChannel News |access-date=2013-12-02 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110616202259/http://www.multichannel.com/article/161701-Disney_XD_Unwraps_On_Friday_The_13_th.php |archive-date=June 16, 2011 }}</ref>

The network replaced [[Toon Disney]], an animation-focused channel that was launched on April 18, 1998, which eventually launched a live-action/animation block called [[Jetix]] in 2004. Jetix channels outside of the United States were relaunched under the Disney XD banner starting with the France-based service on April 1, 2009.<ref>{{cite web |url = http://www.broadbandtvnews.com/?p=14241 |title = French Jetix becomes Disney XD |publisher = Broadband TV News |date = 2009-02-16 |access-date = February 20, 2014 |archive-date = February 18, 2009 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20090218001450/http://www.broadbandtvnews.com/?p=14241 |url-status = live }}</ref> Many of the channel's programs (particularly animated series) previously aired on Toon Disney, mainly as part of the Jetix programming block, which ran on Toon Disney until that channel's shutdown. Disney XD carries the same name as an unrelated mini-site and media player on [[Disney.com]], which stood for '''Disney Xtreme Digital'''.<ref>{{cite news |title=Disney to offer safe social site for kids |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |date=January 17, 2007 |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-157749732.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110624034420/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-157749732.html |archive-date=June 24, 2011 |via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription |url-status=usurped}}</ref> According to Gary Marsh, President of Entertainment for Disney Channel Worldwide, "XD" does not have an actual meaning and was chosen simply because it looked "cool." Marsh notes that "the letter X has many cool associations" and that "the beauty of it is the audience can imbue it with all sorts of positive attributes." "XD" is also an [[emoticon]] for [[laughter]].

The channel's first original television movie, ''[[Skyrunners]]'', premiered on November 27, 2009.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Moody|first1=Annemarie|title=Production Begins on Disney XD's Skyrunners, Weta Handles Creatures|url=http://www.awn.com/news/production-begins-disney-xds-skyrunners-weta-handles-creatures|access-date=November 15, 2014|work=Animation World Network|date=April 7, 2009}}</ref> On April 1, 2012, Disney XD launched a block called "Marvel Universe", as a result of Disney's 2009 acquisition of [[Marvel Entertainment]].<ref name="ut">{{cite news|last1=Truitt|first1=Brian|date=March 7, 2012|title='Ultimate Spider-Man' leads Disney XD's new superhero series|work=AZ Central|publisher=Gannett|agency=USA Today|url=https://www.azcentral.com/thingstodo/kids/articles/20120307ultimate-spider-man-disney-xd-cartoon-series.html|access-date=November 15, 2014|archive-date=November 29, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141129025138/http://www.azcentral.com/thingstodo/kids/articles/20120307ultimate-spider-man-disney-xd-cartoon-series.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> By June 2014, Disney XD agreed to a multi-picture development deal with Two 4 the Money and [[MarVista Entertainment]] with MarVista having global rights.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cummins |first1=Julianna |title=MarVista strikes multi-pic development deal with Disney XD |url=http://kidscreen.com/2014/06/25/production-starts-on-pants-on-fire/ |access-date=June 26, 2019 |work=Kidscreen |agency=Playback Online |publisher=Brunico Communications Ltd. |date=June 25, 2014 |archive-date=June 26, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190626212041/http://kidscreen.com/2014/06/25/production-starts-on-pants-on-fire/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

On November 17, 2016, it was announced that the [[Pokémon (anime)|''Pokémon'']] anime series would be moving to Disney XD from its previous broadcaster, [[Cartoon Network]]. The twentieth season, ''[[List of Pokémon: Sun & Moon episodes|Pokémon the Series: Sun & Moon]]'', was the first season to air and was first broadcast as a sneak peek on December 5, 2016. ''Pokémon'' began its regular broadcast on May 12, 2017.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/olliebarder/2016/11/18/pokemon-sun-and-moon-anime-is-released-on-disney-xd-this-december/#13a1ce464606 |title='Pokémon Sun And Moon' Anime Is Released On Disney XD This December |last=Barder |first=Ollie |newspaper=Forbes |access-date=2016-11-19 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161119120810/http://www.forbes.com/sites/olliebarder/2016/11/18/pokemon-sun-and-moon-anime-is-released-on-disney-xd-this-december/ |archive-date=November 19, 2016 }}</ref>

On August 12, 2017, Disney XD premiered a reboot of ''[[DuckTales (2017 TV series)|DuckTales]]''.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.etonline.com/tv/160267_disney_ducktales_coming_back_to_tv|title=Disney Is Reviving 'DuckTales'!|work=Entertainment Tonight|access-date=2017-11-10|language=en}}</ref> In 2018, through a series of promos and news announcements, Disney announced that four of Disney XD's original series (those being ''[[Star vs. the Forces of Evil]]'', ''[[Milo Murphy's Law]]'', ''[[DuckTales (2017 TV series)|DuckTales]]'', and ''[[Big Hero 6: The Series]]'') would be moving their premieres over to Disney Channel, while Disney XD would continue to air reruns.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2018/02/disney-channel-renews-star-vs-the-forces-of-evil-announces-amphibia-owl-house-1202300438/|title=Disney Channel Launching Toon Series 'Amphibia' & 'The Owl House', Renews 'Star Vs. The Forces Of Evil' For Season 4|first1=Erik|last1=Pedersen|date=February 23, 2018|access-date=February 19, 2020|archive-date=July 24, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200724175959/https://deadline.com/2018/02/disney-channel-renews-star-vs-the-forces-of-evil-announces-amphibia-owl-house-1202300438/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/rebooted-ducktales-moves-to-disney-channel-with-trailer-for-new-episodes|title=Rebooted Ducktales moves to Disney Channel with trailer for new episodes|first=Henry|last=Barajas|date=April 11, 2018|website=SYFY WIRE|access-date=February 19, 2020|archive-date=April 14, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180414000350/http://www.syfy.com/syfywire/rebooted-ducktales-moves-to-disney-channel-with-trailer-for-new-episodes|url-status=dead}}</ref> In 2020, ''DuckTales'' and ''Big Hero 6: The Series'' both returned to Disney XD for their final seasons, both of which ended in 2021.

==Programming==
{{Main|List of programs broadcast by Disney XD}}
Disney XD's schedule consists largely of animated and live-action programs aimed at pre-teens and young teenagers. Disney XD content is a mixture of former original series as well as programs inherited from sister network Disney Channel. In addition to full-length live-action and animated original series, the channel also airs short series similar to those seen on Disney Channel during commercial breaks (such as ''[[Two More Eggs]]''), which serve as filler for programs scheduled to end during the half-hour and last usually around one to three minutes.

In addition, Disney XD airs [[List of Disney Channel original films|Disney Channel Original Movies]] and theatrically released feature films.

Unlike Disney Channel (and similarly, fellow sister network [[Disney Jr.]]), whose advertising comes in the form of [[Promo (media)|program promotions]], [[underwriting|underwriter sponsorships]], and interstitials for Disney films, [[home video]], and game releases produced by the channel,<ref name="Disney No-Ads">[https://variety.com/2003/tv/features/now-that-duff-s-had-enough-1117887469/ Now that Duff's had enough...: is it time for Disney Channel to cash in and rethink no-ads strategy?], ''[[Variety (magazine)|Daily Variety]]'', June 6, 2003. Retrieved April 15, 2013.</ref> Disney XD operates as an advertiser-supported service running traditional [[Television advertisement|television commercials]] in addition to promotions for the channel's shows.

===Sports===
Sister network [[ESPN]] has produced sports-oriented content for Disney XD; for a period, the channel produced an interstitial program known as the ''[[SportsCenter]] High-5'', which featured a countdown of sports highlights. As part of a deal with ESPN, Disney XD carried coverage of the [[2018 Overwatch League season|2018 Overwatch League]] as part of its ''D|XP'' block.<ref name="ESPN-2018" />

In [[2019 Pro Bowl|2019]], Disney XD began to air the [[National Football League|NFL]]'s [[Pro Bowl]], in a simulcast alongside ESPN and [[ESPN on ABC|ABC]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yoder |first=Matt |date=2019-01-24 |title=ESPN is televising the Pro Bowl on four different networks, including Disney XD |url=https://awfulannouncing.com/espn/espn-pro-bowl-four-networks-disney-xd.html |access-date=2023-03-15 |website=Awful Announcing |language=en-US |archive-date=March 15, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230315040714/https://awfulannouncing.com/espn/espn-pro-bowl-four-networks-disney-xd.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

In March 2023, the channel participated in another sports broadcast as part of [[NHL on ESPN|ESPN's NHL coverage]], with Disney Channel and Disney XD airing a 3D animated, ''[[Big City Greens]]''-themed presentation of a game.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Steinberg |first=Brian |date=2023-02-14 |title=Disney Will Animate ESPN Coverage for Disney Channel to Get Kids Into Hockey |url=https://variety.com/2023/tv/news/disney-espn-animated-nhl-hockey-big-city-greens-1235522327/ |access-date=2023-03-15 |website=Variety |language=en-US |archive-date=February 14, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230214170044/https://variety.com/2023/tv/news/disney-espn-animated-nhl-hockey-big-city-greens-1235522327/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Rangers, Capitals in 'Big City Greens' Classic on ESPN, Disney Channel |url=https://www.nhl.com/news/new-york-washington-to-play-in-big-city-greens-classic-on-espn-disney--341043372 |access-date=2023-02-15 |website=NHL.com |language=en-US |archive-date=March 15, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230315050903/https://www.nhl.com/news/new-york-washington-to-play-in-big-city-greens-classic-on-espn-disney-channel/c-341043372 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Programming blocks===
* '''''Randomation Animation''''' – ''Randomation Animation'' was a morning animation block on Saturdays from 8:00 to 11:00&nbsp;a.m. Eastern Time, which debuted on July 13, 2013. Programs featured in the block included ''[[Pac-Man and the Ghostly Adventures]]'', ''[[Xiaolin Chronicles]]'', ''[[Packages from Planet X]]'', ''[[Camp Lakebottom]]'', ''[[Max Steel (2013 TV series)|Max Steel]]'', ''[[Randy Cunningham: 9th Grade Ninja]]'', and ''[[Phineas and Ferb]]''.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Wolfe|first1=Jennifer|title=Disney XD Kicks Off 'Randomation' Block|url=http://www.awn.com/news/disney-xd-kicks-randomation-block|access-date=August 5, 2014|work=Animation World Network|date=July 12, 2013|archive-date=August 12, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140812224803/http://www.awn.com/news/disney-xd-kicks-randomation-block|url-status=live}}</ref>
* '''''Show Me the Monday''''' – ''Show Me the Monday'' was a Monday night programming block that premiered in late 2013. The block presented new episodes of Disney XD original series such as ''[[Lab Rats (American TV series)|Lab Rats]]'', ''[[Mighty Med]]'', ''[[Gravity Falls]]'' and others.
* '''''Animacation''''' – ''Animacation'' was a morning animation block that aired weekdays from 10:00&nbsp;a.m. to 1:00&nbsp;p.m. during the summer of 2014. The block featured new episodes and reruns of original animated programming, as well as the premieres of ''[[Doraemon: Gadget Cat from the Future]]'', ''[[Boyster]]'' and ''[[The 7D]]''.
* '''''D{{vbar}}XP''''' – a prime-time block that featured video gaming-related programming aimed at teens; the block was introduced on July 15, 2017, and ran from 9:00&nbsp;p.m. to. 3:00&nbsp;a.m. Eastern Time. The block draws upon resources from sister properties, such as the Disney-owned [[multi-channel network]] [[Disney Digital Network|Maker Studios]] (who produced the weekly ''Polaris Primetime'', and curates content from its member personalities for other programming on the block), [[ESPN]] (for [[e-sports]] coverage), and [[Vice Media]]'s ''Waypoint'', as well as outside producers and sources such as [[IGN]].<ref>{{cite news|last1=Lafayette|first1=Jon|title=Disney XD Set to Launch Gamer Programming Block|url=http://www.broadcastingcable.com/news/currency/disney-xd-set-launch-gamer-programming-block/166529|access-date=July 14, 2017|work=Broadcasting & Cable|date=June 14, 2017|archive-date=July 17, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170717041217/http://www.broadcastingcable.com/news/currency/disney-xd-set-launch-gamer-programming-block/166529|url-status=live}}</ref> On July 16, ''D{{vbar}}XP'' presented coverage of the finals of the ''[[Super Smash Bros. for Nintendo 3DS and Wii U|Super Smash Bros. for Wii U]]'' and ''[[Street Fighter V]]'' tournaments at [[Evo 2017]], complementing coverage of the event across ESPN networks and [[Twitch (service)|Twitch]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.usgamer.net/articles/disney-xd-will-stream-the-super-smash-bros-finals|title=Disney XD Will Air the Super Smash Bros. Finals|last=Kim|first=Matt|website=USgamer|date=July 14, 2017|language=en-US|access-date=2017-07-17|archive-date=July 17, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170717032250/http://www.usgamer.net/articles/disney-xd-will-stream-the-super-smash-bros-finals|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="disneyxd1">{{cite web|last1=Casey|title=Disney XD Will Be Airing The Super Smash Bros. Wii U Finals From EVO 2017|url=http://www.siliconera.com/2017/07/14/disney-xd-will-airing-super-smash-bros-wii-u-finals-evo-2017/|website=[[Siliconera]]|date=July 14, 2017|access-date=2017-07-14|archive-date=November 7, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171107024803/http://www.siliconera.com/2017/07/14/disney-xd-will-airing-super-smash-bros-wii-u-finals-evo-2017/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="disneyxd2">{{cite web|last1=Beck|first1=Kellen|title=Disney is bringing esports to TV this weekend|url=https://mashable.com/article/disney-esports|website=[[Mashable]]|date=July 14, 2017|access-date=2017-07-14|archive-date=November 16, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171116023724/http://mashable.com/2017/07/14/disney-esports/#RAIJfecY4Pq7|url-status=live}}</ref> On July 11, 2018, [[ESPN]] announced a multi-year deal with [[Blizzard Entertainment]] to air [[Overwatch League]] matches on [[American Broadcasting Company|ABC]], Disney XD (as a part of the ''D{{vbar}}XP'' block) and the ESPN networks, beginning with the [[2018 Overwatch League season|2018 playoffs]].<ref name="ESPN-2018">{{cite web|url=http://www.espn.com/esports/story/_/id/24062274/overwatch-league-comes-espn-disney-abc|title=Overwatch League comes to ESPN, Disney and ABC|publisher=[[ESPN]]|date=July 11, 2018|access-date=July 11, 2018|archive-date=July 11, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180711180039/http://www.espn.com/esports/story/_/id/24062274/overwatch-league-comes-espn-disney-abc|url-status=live}}</ref>
* '''''Anime Block''''' – a block showcasing various [[anime]] programs featured on the channel, such as ''[[Pokémon (TV series)|Pokémon]]'', ''[[Yo-kai Watch]]'' and ''[[Beyblade Burst]]''.<ref>{{cite AV media |date=Feb 24, 2017 |title=Anime Block: Pokémon, Beyblade, Yo-kai Watch |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ds6UUL57tCM |access-date=July 14, 2017 |location=YouTube |publisher=Disney XD }}</ref> It launched on February 18, 2017, and aired every Saturday.<ref>{{cite web|title=Split screens and exaggerated expressions mean only one thing - the Anime Block is coming to Disney XD this Saturday|url=https://twitter.com/disneyxd/status/832673534132396033?lang=en|website=Twitter|publisher=Disney XD|access-date=July 15, 2017|date=February 17, 2017|archive-date=October 8, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231008211924/https://twitter.com/disneyxd/status/832673534132396033?lang=en|url-status=live}}</ref> It was discontinued in 2020 following [[Netflix]]'s acquisition of the streaming rights to ''Pokémon''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Alexander |first=Julia |date=2020-04-23 |title=Netflix nabs exclusive Pokémon episodes in latest move to dominate children’s television |url=https://www.theverge.com/2020/4/23/21232750/netflix-pokemon-exclusive-episodes-disney-hbo-max-kids-tv-anime-streaming-wars |access-date=2025-12-13 |website=The Verge |language=en-US}}</ref>
* '''''{{visible anchor|Marvel on Disney XD}}''''' – a block of animated series produced by [[Marvel Animation]] that aired Sunday mornings from 8:00 to 9:30&nbsp;a.m. Eastern Time, which resulted from The Walt Disney Company's 2009 acquisition of [[Marvel Entertainment]]. The block launched on April 1, 2012, as ''Marvel Universe'' with the premiere of ''[[Ultimate Spider-Man (TV series)|Ultimate Spider-Man]]'', followed by returning series ''[[The Avengers: Earth's Mightiest Heroes]]''. It also featured five short series using short-form animated and live-action interstitials.<ref name="ut" /> Programs that were featured in the block included [[Spider-Man (2017 TV series)|''Spider-Man'' (2017)]],<ref>{{Cite web|title='Ultimate Spider-Man' Cartoon to Kick Off Disney XD Marvel Programming Block April 1|url=http://comicsalliance.com/ultimate-spider-man-cartoon-disney-xd-marvel-programming-block-april-1/|access-date=2017-11-10|website=ComicsAlliance|date=January 10, 2012|archive-date=September 12, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170912102615/http://comicsalliance.com/ultimate-spider-man-cartoon-disney-xd-marvel-programming-block-april-1/|url-status=live}}</ref> ''[[Avengers Assemble (TV series)|Avengers Assemble]]'', ''[[Guardians of the Galaxy (TV series)|Guardians of the Galaxy]]'',<ref name="cbr">{{Cite news|date=May 23, 2013|title=Marvel Calls "Avengers Assemble" On DisneyXD|url=http://www.comicbookresources.com:8080/?page=article&id=45663|url-status=dead|access-date=June 9, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130927114011/http://www.comicbookresources.com:8080/?page=article&id=45663|archive-date=September 27, 2013|df=mdy-all}}</ref> "Marvel Mash-Up" (which features classic Marvel cartoons mashed up with new twists) and "Fury Files".

